\textbf{Chapter 1 -- Introduction}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{1.1 Background}

Campus network, or also known as the campus area network, is a
proprietary local area network (LAN) or a set of multiple interconnected
LANs owned by a specific organization such as university, corporate or
hospital (Contributor, 2025). In general, the campus network could span
different geographical areas, from linking multiple university faculties
in close distance to connecting headquarter and branch offices which are
geographically dispersed. According to Upravnik (2022)\textbf{,} a
campus network is typically designed using a three-tier hierarchical
model, which involves the core layer, aggregation or distribution layer
and finally the access layer for the purpose of scalability, reliability
and security.

Each layer comes with its own specialized functions, for instance, the
core layer is used to provide high-speed routing as well as connect to
other parts of the network like internet, network management zone, data
center, demilitarized zone and other campus branches. The aggregation
layer, which connects the core layer to the access layer, is used to
aggregate traffic from the access layer, perform the routing and
filtering between different network segments, as well as enforce QOS and
security policies. Lastly, the access layer, being the layer closest to
the end-user, provides wired and wireless connection to terminals, like
desktops, phones, wireless access points and IoT devices (\emph{Access,
Distribution and Core Layers Explained}, 2025).
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=4.19861in,height=2.64583in]{vertopal_de581486deb04d809884e452fca13b13/media/image1.png}

\emph{Figure 1: Three-tier Hierarchical Model (Upravnik, 2022)}

\begin{quote}
With many devices and systems joining in the network, the campus
networks have grown in scale and complexity, causing the network
administrators to have a hard time to manage these devices (Huawei
Technologies Co., Ltd., 2025). To work this out, the Network Management
Zone is connected to the core layer switches, giving the administrators
a central view of the whole network topology as well as allowing them to
perform centralized network device administration and monitoring (Huawei
Technologies Co., Ltd., n.d.). Nowadays, many vendors have also provided
their management platform solution to support centralized network
administration, for instance, Cisco DNA center, Juniper Mist and Huawei
NCE.

However, these platforms fall short in respective different aspects. For
example, most proprietary ecosystems are closed, others lacking
multi-vendor interoperability and almost all of them are too expensive
to implement due to high-priced licence (\emph{Cisco DNA Center Reviews
\& Product Details}, n.d.). Not only that, there also exists an obvious
gap between operator's intention and the actual network commands in
today's existing open-source and proprietary vendor platform. For
example, an operator may want to isolate a department's network, but to
do that, they need to manually write multiple vendor-specific
configurations or navigate through different vendor tools. This process
could require the operator to have vast knowledge of different vendor
commands and syntaxes, not to mention it could possibly lead to errors,
inconsistencies and delays. Although some automation tools exist, many
are expensive and only support specific vendors.
\end{quote}

\includegraphics[width=4.45833in,height=3.5625in]{vertopal_de581486deb04d809884e452fca13b13/media/image2.png}

\begin{quote}
\emph{Figure 2: Network Management Zone Connected to Core Layer Switches
(Huawei Technologies Co., Ltd., n.d.)}

This project proposes \textbf{CogniShield}, an LLM-assisted campus
network configuration and automation platform that uses natural language
instructions to abstract vendor-specific syntax differences and convert
them into Ansible-based automation playbooks. Instead of fully
replicating enterprise platforms such as Cisco DNA Center or Huawei
iMaster NCE, CogniShield focuses on selected features such as
multi-vendor configuration abstraction, configuration generation and
controlled deployment across selected network devices. The system
emphasizes Day-1 and Day-2 automation, where devices are already
reachable and supports human validation before critical changes are
executed. CogniShield will be developed as an open-source and
vendor-neutral platform to make network automation more accessible for
universities, small enterprises and research institutions without
requiring expensive licenses or proprietary hardware.

\textbf{1.2 Problem Statements}

In today's world, there are numerous complex network automation systems
used in campus or enterprise networks, where most of which are owned by
well-known companies such as Cisco, Juniper, Aruba and Huawei. Despite
the fact that these platforms are quite robust, for instance Cisco DNA
Center, Juniper Mist and Huawei iMaster NCE, they all have one thing in
common, which is they are not widely accessible or open to the public
(\emph{Open Networking Alternative to Cisco, Juniper, Arista, Nokia},
2025). The primary reason here is due to their proprietary licensing
system. In this context, licenses, software subscriptions and hardware
are all extremely expensive, making them tough for small and
medium-sized businesses, startups and even certain institutions to
purchase (\emph{Cisco DNA Center Reviews \& Product Details}, n.d.).
What is more distressing is that there are no other viable open-source
alternatives for the network community. Such circumstances present a
significant challenge for individuals who cannot afford or wish to
construct their own configurable network automation or zero-trust system
without relying on a provider. As a result, the organization will have
to invest more time, money and labor in the traditional manual
configuration and monitoring of its network. Moreover, the proprietary
licensing also affects students, researchers and network hobbyists by
restraining them from investigating or learning how these network
automation tools work. And because the majority of the resources and
underlying architecture are restricted and not released publicly, it is
difficult to conduct community reviews or collaborative development with
the public (\emph{Free and Open Source Software in Education \textbar{}
Research Starters \textbar{} EBSCO Research}, n.d.). As a result,
innovation faces stagnation and open, low-budget, vendor-neutral network
automation systems struggle to come into existence.

Aside from that, another issue in the current network field is that,
while there are many automation tools or management platforms available,
the majority of them still lack practical LLM-assisted features that
help convert network engineers\textquotesingle{} natural language
instructions into usable device configurations and automation playbooks.
For example, if a network administrator wishes to segregate one
department\textquotesingle s network, they must still manually write
commands or navigate through multiple interfaces such as Command Line
Interface (CLI), Application Programming Interface (API), or Graphical
User Interface (GUI) across various vendor devices to implement firewall
rules (5 Intent-Based Networking Vendors, n.d.). This

manual method takes a long time and requires extensive technical
knowledge of each vendor\textquotesingle s system and configuration
syntaxes. As the number of devices increases and the network
infrastructure becomes increasingly complex, such manual processes
become inefficient and may introduce human errors such as configuration
drift or inconsistency. Ultimately, this significantly reduces the
overall system\textquotesingle s flexibility, maintainability, and
scalability (``Transforming Network Configuration Management: Challenges
and Solutions,'' 2022).

\textbf{1.3 Motivation}

It was my experience during an internship at Huawei Malaysia that
inspired me to start this project. Back then, I was assigned many
repetitive network tasks. I realized that most of these tasks followed
predefined routines and could therefore be automated. Since then, I have
written many automation scripts to facilitate the workflow, which saved
a significant amount of time and effort. Later on, what truly sparked my
interest in doing this automation project was getting to see how their
internal network management platform, Huawei iMaster NCE, works and
looks like. From the platform, I got a glimpse of how network
configurations and administration, which I had learned in the CST337
course instructed by Dr. Chong Yung Wey, could be simplified and
automated in a real-world environment. On top of that, I was also
fortunate to be given the opportunity to study for and take the Huawei
Certified ICT Professional (HCIP) Network Automation Developer course
and examination, where I learned a vast amount of knowledge on
automation technologies, concepts, and tools. This further exposed me to
the possibilities of automation and AI-assisted configuration management
in networking.

Given this internship exposure and certification opportunity, I decided
to explore this technology more deeply. That was when I realized that
much of this technology is largely locked within proprietary vendor
ecosystems and is not easily accessible to everyone. Surprisingly, there
are still very limited open-source systems that allow students,
universities, and smaller organizations to gain hands-on experience with
network automation. That was when I came up with the idea of creating an
open-source network configuration and automation platform specifically
for campus and SME environments. I decided to start this project after
realizing how much time and effort network administrators could save by
automating repetitive configuration tasks and reducing dependency on
vendor-specific syntax. This tool could be highly beneficial for small
enterprises such as startups, small institutions, students, and
researchers who cannot afford expensive commercial platforms and who
would like to customize the tools according to their own needs. I want
to build something that makes network automation easier, faster, and
more accessible for everyone without heavy licensing restrictions. With
CogniShield, I aim to simplify multi-vendor network configuration by
using natural language to generate automation playbooks while keeping
the system affordable, practical, and suitable for educational and
research purposes.

\textbf{1.4 System Objectives}

1.\hspace{0pt} To develop an open-source and vendor-neutral network
configuration automation platform that improves accessibility for SMEs,
universities and research communities.\hspace{0pt}

2.\hspace{0pt} To implement an LLM-assisted network automation system
that uses natural language instructions to abstract vendor-specific
syntax differences and generate Ansible playbooks for multi-vendor
device configuration.\hspace{0pt}

3.\hspace{0pt} To support controlled Day-1 and Day-2 network automation,
including configuration generation, validation and deployment while
maintaining human-in-the-loop approval for critical changes.

\textbf{1.5 Proposed Solutions}

In hope of solving issues faced in the problem statement, this project
proposes a new concept called CogniShield, an LLM-assisted network
configuration automation platform powered by the Large Language Model
(LLM). It is neither a simple network management tool nor a closed
commercialized platform, but an AI-driven open-source automation system,
aiming to provide a free community alternative for selected automation
features found in existing vendor platforms. Instead of fully mimicking
the whole functionality of Cisco DNA Center, Huawei iMaster NCE, or
Juniper Mist, this project focuses on improving selected features such
as natural-language-assisted configuration generation, vendor syntax
abstraction, and Ansible-based deployment across selected network
devices. Ultimately, this network automation platform shall be connected
to the campus network and placed at the network management zone as shown
in Figure 3.

To begin with, the entire concept of CogniShield is based on open-system
architecture, in which the whole system is built upon open-source
components and does not require expensive software subscriptions or
proprietary hardware components to run it. With that being said, the
system can run and function seamlessly on top of standard servers with
the help of virtualization technology such as Proxmox and virtual
machines (Proxmox, n.d.). All these ensure that small-to-medium
enterprises, startups, universities, and research institutes can also
build and use the system without needing to worry too much about
hardware costs, licensing fees, and vendor restrictions.

From the perspective of the architecture, the system is made up of
stable open-source components such as NetBox for network device
inventory management and Ansible for automation deployment (Ansible
Documentation --- Ansible Community Documentation, n.d.). In terms of
monitoring and visualization, CogniShield would use SNMP, Telemetry and
to perform monitoring, InfluxDB for time-series databases
(\emph{Overview \textbar{} Prometheus}, n.d.; InfluxDB OSS V2
Documentation, n.d.). In this project, open architecture is highly
emphasized with the goal of promoting the cost-saving, flexibility and
interoperability, not to mention allowing the enterprises or users to
develop and customize the system based on their environment and
operational needs. For students, researchers and community developers,
the system serves more like a free experimental field, where they could
explore, innovate and

share their research together on this open platform, contributing their
findings and promoting community-driven development.

Among all the components integrated, the Large Language Model (LLM) is
the core of CogniShield, where it helps interpret natural language
instructions and bridge the gap between network engineer requests and
vendor-specific device configurations. For example, when the network
administrator provides an instruction such as ``Configure OSPF process 1
on router 1, router 2, and router 3 in area 0'' or ``Create static
routing from router 1 to router 2 using the next-hop address,'' the
system generates the corresponding automation scripts in the form of
Ansible playbooks based on the selected vendor and device information.
This process reduces the complexity of manually writing multi-vendor
configuration scripts, minimizes human error, and enables operators
without extensive vendor-specific knowledge to manage network
configurations more efficiently.

Another important point is that regardless of how much automation the
system provides, CogniShield is only meant to assist engineers in
generating and deploying network configurations rather than acting as a
fully autonomous AI network engineer. When it comes to critical network
changes, the generated configurations still need to undergo manual
review and approval before actual implementation. Human intervention
remains necessary because LLM responses may not always be fully accurate
and could potentially generate unsafe or incorrect configuration
commands. That being said, this system ultimately serves as an assistant
tool to ease configuration generation and deployment workflow rather
than a fully autonomous network controller that acts on its own.

\includegraphics[width=10.29167in,height=5.09306in]{vertopal_de581486deb04d809884e452fca13b13/media/image3.png}
\end{quote}

\emph{Figure 3: Integration of CogniShield to Campus Network in
